id: k8s.headless-service
kind: kubernetes
title: Headless Service
aliases: headless service, clusterIP None
tags: networking, discovery, stateful
questions:
  - why does a StatefulSet need a headless service?
  - what changes when you choose a StatefulSet over a Deployment?
invariant: With clusterIP None there is no virtual IP and no load balancing; DNS returns the Pod IPs directly.
answer_30s: A headless Service publishes per-Pod DNS records instead of a single virtual IP, which is what gives each StatefulSet replica an addressable name.
answer_2m: |
  `db-0.db.default.svc.cluster.local` resolves to that specific Pod. Clients
  that need to address individual replicas (replication, quorum, sharding) get
  what they need; clients that just need to reach a random replica get the
  normal ClusterIP Service. Both are commonly deployed over the same Pods at
  once -- headless for peer traffic, ClusterIP for client traffic.
when_to_use:
  - StatefulSet peer discovery, custom client-side load balancing
avoid_when:
  - plain request distribution (the client would have to load balance itself)
related:
  - k8s.stateful-set
  - k8s.dns-service-discovery
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#headless-services
